1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,388
Rods of copper, steel and glass, all the same size, are heated at one end.

2
00:00:04,388 --> 00:00:08,308
A metal pin is stuck to the far end of each rod with a little wax.

3
00:00:08,308 --> 00:00:12,287
Predict: on which rod will the pin fall off first? Pause and decide.

4
00:00:13,346 --> 00:00:18,027
Watch. After about 40 seconds the wax on the copper rod melts and its pin falls.

5
00:00:19,085 --> 00:00:24,117
The steel pin falls after about 3 minutes. The pin on the glass rod is still in place.

6
00:00:25,175 --> 00:00:28,979
Explain what you saw. Heat travels along the rods by conduction.

7
00:00:28,979 --> 00:00:33,133
Particles at the hot end vibrate faster and pass the vibrations along.

8
00:00:33,133 --> 00:00:39,218
Metals like copper also have free electrons that carry energy quickly, so copper is the best conductor.

9
00:00:39,218 --> 00:00:45,596
Glass has no free electrons, so it is a poor conductor: an insulator. Pause and explain it in your own words.

10
00:00:46,654 --> 00:00:49,580
Conduction transfers heat through direct contact,

11
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:55,431
mainly in solids: particles at the hot end of a spoon vibrate faster and pass the vibrations along.

12
00:00:55,431 --> 00:00:59,175
Metals conduct well; plastic, wood, wool and air are insulators.

13
00:01:00,234 --> 00:01:05,617
Convection happens in liquids and gases. Heated fluid becomes less dense and rises; cooler,

14
00:01:05,617 --> 00:01:11,819
denser fluid sinks to take its place, making a convection current. That's how a heater warms a whole room.

15
00:01:12,877 --> 00:01:19,197
Radiation transfers heat by infrared waves, with no medium needed: that's how the Sun's heat crosses space.

16
00:01:19,197 --> 00:01:24,287
Dull black surfaces absorb and emit it best; shiny white or silver surfaces reflect it.
